--- `crow run` (compiled) killed by SIGALRM leaves the program it built running.
--- The brief's and rosetta/run.sh's time limit is exactly that signal:
---     perl -e 'alarm shift; exec @ARGV' 6 crow run integer_sequence-1.co
--- crow exits 142 after 6 s, and the compiled program
--- (.crow_build/target/debug/<name>) goes on, reparented to pid 1, until killed
--- by hand. With SIGTERM sent to crow instead, the program is stopped with it
--- (crow exits 1, nothing left). Found running Rosetta Code's Integer sequence
--- endlessly under a 3 s alarm: crow -i stopped, but the compiled count, orphaned,
--- had written 22 GB to its output file before it was found.
--- Expected: crow stops the program it started on SIGALRM (and SIGHUP, SIGINT,
--- SIGQUIT) as it does on SIGTERM, or runs it in its own process group and
--- signals the group. (crow run -i is one process and has no such leftover.)

n = 0
while true
    sleep[200]
    n = n + 1
